# Error Handling Rules

## Error Categories

```typescript
// TEACHING: Distinguish between expected and unexpected errors

// Expected errors - User can fix these
// - Validation failures
// - Resource not found
// - Permission denied
// - Rate limiting

// Unexpected errors - System issues
// - Database connection failed
// - External API down
// - Out of memory
// - Programming bugs
```

## API Error Handling (oRPC)

```typescript
// TEACHING: Return appropriate HTTP status codes
// Clients rely on these to handle errors correctly

import { ORPCError } from "@orpc/server";

export const getEvent = publicProcedure
  .input(z.object({ id: z.string().uuid() }))
  .handler(async ({ input }) => {
    const event = await db.query.event.findFirst({
      where: eq(eventTable.id, input.id),
    });

    // TEACHING: 404 for missing resources
    if (!event) {
      throw new ORPCError({
        code: "NOT_FOUND",
        message: "Event not found",
      });
    }

    // TEACHING: 403 for permission issues
    if (event.isPrivate) {
      throw new ORPCError({
        code: "FORBIDDEN",
        message: "You don't have access to this event",
      });
    }

    return event;
  });
```

## Client-Side Error Handling

```typescript
// TEACHING: Always handle errors gracefully in UI
"use client";

import { toast } from "sonner";

async function handleSubmit(data: FormData) {
  try {
    await createEvent(data);
    toast.success("Event created!");
  } catch (error) {
    // TEACHING: Show user-friendly message, log technical details
    if (error instanceof ORPCError) {
      toast.error(error.message); // User-friendly message from server
    } else {
      toast.error("Something went wrong. Please try again.");
      console.error("Unexpected error:", error); // For debugging
    }
  }
}
```

## Error Boundaries

```typescript
// TEACHING: Error boundaries catch render errors
// Without them, one broken component crashes the whole app

"use client";

import { Component, ReactNode } from "react";

class ErrorBoundary extends Component<
  { children: ReactNode; fallback: ReactNode },
  { hasError: boolean }
> {
  state = { hasError: false };

  static getDerivedStateFromError() {
    return { hasError: true };
  }

  componentDidCatch(error: Error, info: React.ErrorInfo) {
    // TEACHING: Log to error tracking service in production
    console.error("Error boundary caught:", error, info);
  }

  render() {
    if (this.state.hasError) {
      return this.props.fallback;
    }
    return this.props.children;
  }
}

// Usage
<ErrorBoundary fallback={<ErrorFallback />}>
  <RiskyComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

## Logging Best Practices

```typescript
// TEACHING: Logs are for debugging, not user communication

// BAD - Too vague
console.error("Error occurred");

// BAD - Exposes sensitive data
console.error("Login failed for:", { email, password });

// GOOD - Contextual, safe
console.error("Event creation failed:", {
  userId: context.session.user.id,
  eventData: { title: input.title }, // Only non-sensitive fields
  error: error.message,
});

// TEACHING: Use appropriate log levels
console.log()   // General info (development)
console.warn()  // Potential issues
console.error() // Actual errors
```

## Form Validation Errors

```typescript
// TEACHING: Show validation errors inline, near the field

import { useForm } from "react-hook-form";
import { zodResolver } from "@hookform/resolvers/zod";

const form = useForm({
  resolver: zodResolver(EventSchema),
});

// In the form
<FormField
  control={form.control}
  name="title"
  render={({ field, fieldState }) => (
    <FormItem>
      <FormLabel>Title</FormLabel>
      <FormControl>
        <Input {...field} />
      </FormControl>
      {/* TEACHING: Error message appears right below the field */}
      <FormMessage>{fieldState.error?.message}</FormMessage>
    </FormItem>
  )}
/>
```

## Never Do

1. **Never swallow errors** silently (empty catch blocks)
2. **Never expose stack traces** to users
3. **Never log sensitive data** (passwords, tokens, PII)
4. **Never use generic messages** for validation errors
5. **Never blame the user** - say "Invalid email" not "You entered wrong email"
